WEBVTT

00:00.171 --> 00:02.982
- In earlier mathematics
that you may have done,

00:02.982 --> 00:05.789
you probably got familiar
with the idea of a factor.

00:05.789 --> 00:06.926
So for example, let me just pick

00:06.926 --> 00:09.382
an arbitrary number, the number 12.

00:09.382 --> 00:14.382
We could say that the
number 12 is the product of

00:14.552 --> 00:18.730
say two and six; two
times six is equal to 12.

00:18.730 --> 00:19.958
So because if you take the product of

00:19.958 --> 00:21.517
two and six, you get 12, we could say

00:21.517 --> 00:23.837
that two is a factor of
12, we could also say

00:23.837 --> 00:25.151
that six is a factor of 12.

00:25.151 --> 00:27.752
You take the product of
these things and you get 12!

00:27.752 --> 00:32.236
You could even say that
this is 12 in factored form.

00:32.236 --> 00:33.683
People don't really talk that way

00:33.683 --> 00:35.815
but you could think of it that way.

00:35.815 --> 00:39.417
We broke 12 into the things
that we could use to multiply.

00:39.417 --> 00:41.283
And you probably remember
from earlier mathematics

00:41.283 --> 00:43.040
the notion of prime factorization,

00:43.040 --> 00:45.042
where you break it up into
all of the prime factors.

00:45.042 --> 00:46.492
So in that case you could break the six

00:46.492 --> 00:48.294
into a two and a three, and you have

00:48.294 --> 00:53.294
two times two times three is equal to 12.

00:53.697 --> 00:55.625
And you'd say, "Well, this would be 12

00:55.625 --> 01:00.175
"in prime factored form or the
prime factorization of 12,"

01:00.175 --> 01:04.300
so these are the prime factors.

01:04.300 --> 01:08.095
And so the general idea,
this notion of a factor

01:08.095 --> 01:10.798
is things that you can multiply together

01:10.798 --> 01:12.748
to get your original thing.

01:12.762 --> 01:14.852
Or if you're talking about factored form,

01:14.852 --> 01:17.275
you're essentially taking the
number and you're breaking

01:17.275 --> 01:19.029
it up into the things
that when you multiply

01:19.029 --> 01:21.697
them together, you get
your original number.

01:21.697 --> 01:23.076
What we're going to do now is extend

01:23.076 --> 01:25.743
this idea into the algebraic domain.

01:25.743 --> 01:29.196
So if we start with an expression,

01:29.196 --> 01:31.527
let's say the expression is

01:31.527 --> 01:35.552
two plus four X,

01:35.552 --> 01:38.362
can we break this up into the product

01:38.362 --> 01:41.499
of two either numbers or two expressions

01:41.499 --> 01:44.579
or the product of a
number and an expression?

01:44.579 --> 01:47.183
Well, one thing that might jump out at you

01:47.183 --> 01:50.486
is we can write this as

01:50.486 --> 01:55.265
two times one plus two X.

01:55.265 --> 01:57.094
And you can verify if you like

01:57.094 --> 02:00.126
that this does indeed
equal two plus four X.

02:00.126 --> 02:02.588
We're just going to distribute the two.

02:02.588 --> 02:07.422
Two times one is two, two times two X

02:07.422 --> 02:11.820
is equal to four X, so plus four X.

02:11.820 --> 02:15.992
So in our algebra brains, this will often

02:15.992 --> 02:18.487
be reviewed as or referred to as

02:18.487 --> 02:22.644
this expression factored
or in a factored form.

02:22.644 --> 02:23.935
Sometimes people would say that

02:23.935 --> 02:26.893
we have factored out the two.

02:26.893 --> 02:28.305
You could just as easily say that

02:28.305 --> 02:30.372
you have factored out a one plus two X.

02:30.372 --> 02:35.266
You have broken this thing
up into two of its factors.

02:35.266 --> 02:37.194
So let's do a couple of examples

02:37.194 --> 02:38.882
of this and then we'll
think about, you know,

02:38.882 --> 02:40.787
I just told you that we
could write it this way

02:40.787 --> 02:42.730
but how do you actually figure that out?

02:42.730 --> 02:44.731
So let's do another one.

02:44.731 --> 02:47.147
Let's say that you had, I don't know,

02:47.147 --> 02:51.832
let's say you had, six, let
me just in a different color,

02:51.847 --> 02:56.127
let's say you had six X

02:56.127 --> 03:01.127
six X plus three,

03:01.396 --> 03:03.204
no, let's write it

03:03.204 --> 03:06.484
six X plus 30, that's interesting.

03:06.484 --> 03:08.549
So one way to think about it is

03:08.549 --> 03:11.186
can we break up each of these terms

03:11.186 --> 03:13.809
so that they have a common factor?

03:13.809 --> 03:17.353
Well, this one over here,
six X literally represents

03:17.353 --> 03:22.126
six times X, and then 30,
if I want to break out

03:22.126 --> 03:24.346
a six, 30 is divisible
by six, so I could write

03:24.346 --> 03:27.549
this as six times five,

03:27.549 --> 03:30.017
30 is the same thing as six times five.

03:30.017 --> 03:31.330
And when you write it this way,

03:31.330 --> 03:34.237
you see, "Hey, I can factor out a six!"

03:34.237 --> 03:35.703
Essentially, this is the reverse

03:35.703 --> 03:37.657
of the distributive property!

03:37.657 --> 03:40.521
So I'm essentially undoing
the distributive property,

03:40.521 --> 03:43.699
taking out the six, and you
are going to end up with,

03:43.699 --> 03:48.699
so if you take out the six,
you end up with six times,

03:49.823 --> 03:51.316
so if you take out the six here,

03:51.316 --> 03:52.805
you have an X, and you take out

03:52.805 --> 03:55.239
the six here, you have plus five.

03:55.239 --> 03:57.628
So six X plus 30, if you factor it,

03:57.628 --> 04:00.815
we could write it as
six times X plus five.

04:00.815 --> 04:02.985
And you can verify with
the distributive property.

04:02.985 --> 04:05.491
If you distribute this six, you get

04:05.491 --> 04:09.005
six X + five times six or six X + 30.

04:09.005 --> 04:09.927
Let's do something that's a little bit

04:09.927 --> 04:11.419
more interesting where we might

04:11.419 --> 04:13.770
want to factor out a fraction.

04:13.770 --> 04:16.290
So let's say we had the situation ...

04:16.290 --> 04:18.652
Let me get a new color here.

04:18.652 --> 04:20.404
So let's say we had

04:20.404 --> 04:25.187
1/2 minus 3/2,

04:25.187 --> 04:27.293
minus 3/2 X.

04:27.293 --> 04:29.541
How could we write this in a, I guess

04:29.541 --> 04:31.245
you could say, in a factored form,

04:31.245 --> 04:33.573
or if we wanted to factor out something?

04:33.573 --> 04:34.913
I encourage you to pause the video

04:34.913 --> 04:37.324
and try to figure it out,
and I'll give you a hint.

04:37.324 --> 04:39.730
See if you can factor out 1/2.

04:39.960 --> 04:41.559
Let's write it that way.

04:41.559 --> 04:42.845
If we're trying to factor out 1/2,

04:42.845 --> 04:44.816
we can write this first term as

04:44.816 --> 04:47.851
1/2 times one and this second one

04:47.851 --> 04:52.851
we could write as minus 1/2 times three X.

04:53.578 --> 04:55.956
That's what this is,
3/2 X is the same thing

04:55.956 --> 04:59.360
as three X divided by
two or 1/2 times three X.

04:59.360 --> 05:01.345
And then here we can see
that we can just factor

05:01.345 --> 05:03.520
out the 1/2 and you're going to get

05:03.520 --> 05:08.233
1/2 times one minus three X.

05:08.233 --> 05:10.560
Another way you could
have thought about it is,

05:10.560 --> 05:13.017
"Hey, look, both of these are products

05:13.017 --> 05:15.376
"involving 1/2," and
that's a little bit more

05:15.376 --> 05:18.428
confusing when you're
dealing with a fraction here.

05:18.428 --> 05:20.119
But one way to think about it is,

05:20.119 --> 05:23.252
I can divide out a 1/2
from each of these terms.

05:23.252 --> 05:25.787
So if I divide out a 1/2 from this,

05:25.787 --> 05:28.253
1/2 divided by 1/2 is one.

05:28.253 --> 05:31.267
And if I take 3/2 and divide it by 1/2,

05:31.267 --> 05:33.751
that's going to be
three, and so I took out

05:33.751 --> 05:35.798
a 1/2, that's another
way to think about it.

05:35.798 --> 05:37.287
I don't know if that confuses you more

05:37.287 --> 05:38.893
or it confuses you less, but hopefully

05:38.893 --> 05:42.808
this gives you the sense of
what factoring an expression is.

05:42.808 --> 05:44.911
I'll do another example,
where we're even using

05:44.911 --> 05:49.911
more abstract things, so
I could say, "AX plus AY."

05:50.705 --> 05:52.929
How could we write this in factored form?

05:52.929 --> 05:55.309
Well, both of these terms have products

05:55.309 --> 06:00.309
of A in it, so I could write
this as A times X plus Y.

06:00.554 --> 06:02.625
And sometimes you'll hear people say,

06:02.625 --> 06:04.981
"You have factored out the A,"

06:04.981 --> 06:07.532
and you can verify it if
you multiply this out again.

06:07.532 --> 06:11.903
If you distribute the A,
you'd be left with AX plus AY.
